<!--
  This is a scaffold, not a finished file.

  What this is: once it is filled in and saved as CLAUDE.md, Claude reads
  it at the start of every session and follows what it says. A rule you
  put here is one you do not have to repeat.

  The easy way — let Claude set it up: tell Claude "set up Hold My
  Rules". That runs Hold My Rules setup: Claude asks you a few short
  questions, then writes the file for you, names it CLAUDE.md, puts it in
  the right place inside Claude, and sets it to load every session — so
  you can just get to work. You do not have to touch this scaffold
  yourself. Claude asks before creating the file and confirms where it
  went.

  By hand instead:
  1. Decide where it should live (see "Advanced: where to put it" below)
     and copy it there.
  2. Rename it to  CLAUDE.md  (drop the ".example").
  3. Fill in the sections below.

  Each section has a short guidance note and a few example lines. Copy
  any example you like up under the heading and edit it to fit; delete
  the rest. Keep it short — a page or so is plenty.

  The examples are illustrations, not defaults and not recommendations.

  ── ADVANCED ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  You can skip everything below. If you let Claude set this up, it
  handles all of it for you.

  Where to put it — your rules can apply to all your work or to just one
  project:
  - All your work (every project):  ~/.claude/CLAUDE.md
  - Just this project (and anyone who opens it):  a file named CLAUDE.md
    in the project's top folder

  Rule of thumb: rules about how you like Claude to work -> make them
  apply to all your work; rules about one specific project -> save them
  in that project; not sure? -> Claude asks. If both files exist, Claude
  reads both.

  In this scaffold, "Working style" and "Output requirements" usually
  belong in the all-your-work file; "Purpose", "Audience", and "Scope
  and decision rules" are usually project-specific.

  Either way, what you write here becomes the standing context for the
  session. It raises the chance your rules are followed. It is not a
  guarantee, and it is not a substitute for saying what you need in the
  moment.

  Auto-load: add the line below so the Hold My Rules skill loads every
  session. You can also just ask Claude to "make Hold My Rules
  automatic" and it will add this for you.

      Use the hold-my-rules skill.

  Self-named file: if you would rather keep your rules in a file with
  its own name, save them as  hold-my-rules.md  and put this line in
  CLAUDE.md instead:

      @hold-my-rules.md
  ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
-->

# Project rules

## Purpose
<!-- What is this project? What are you trying to produce or achieve?

     Example:
     - A weekly customer newsletter for a small software company.
-->


## Audience
<!-- Who is the output for? What do they already know? What do they
     expect from it?

     Example:
     - Non-technical customers. No jargon. They want the point up front.
-->


## Working style
<!-- How should Claude work with you? How much to explain, when to ask
     before acting versus proceed, how much initiative to take, how to
     handle uncertainty. State your own preferences.

     Examples:
     - Be direct and concise by default.
     - Lead with the recommendation or answer.
     - When unsure, make a reasonable call and flag it rather than
       stopping to ask.
-->


## Output requirements
<!-- Any fixed requirements for what Claude produces: format, structure,
     file types, naming, length, voice, things to always or never
     include. Leave blank if you have none.

     Examples:
     - Use tables for meaningful comparisons.
     - Do not use emojis unless I ask.
     - Make the tone sound natural.
-->


## Scope and decision rules
<!-- What is in scope and out of scope? Which decisions are already made
     and should not be reopened? What must Claude check with you about
     before doing it?

     Examples:
     - Ask before making broad changes to existing files.
     - The visual redesign is out of scope for now.
-->


## Editing and preservation rules
<!-- When changing existing files: what must be left untouched? Specific
     sections, wording, formatting, structure, formulas, or data? How
     small should changes be by default?

     Examples:
     - When editing, preserve unrelated content and explain meaningful
       changes.
     - Make the smallest change that does the job.
-->


## Definition of done
<!-- How do you know a task is finished and correct? What should Claude
     check before telling you it is done?

     Example:
     - Re-read the result against these rules, then give me a one-line
       summary of what changed.
-->
